# hbsetbg

The tool used to set the desktop background in Hackedbox.

It loads an image and applies it to the desktop/root window using one of four placement modes. It is intended specifically for managing the Hackedbox desktop background.

Usage:
hbsetbg [option] <path to image>

## Options

-center, --center <image>
    Display the image at its original size, centered on the screen.

-tile, --tile <image>
    Tile the image across the entire screen.

-s2c, --stretch2center <image>
    Scale the image proportionally so that it fits within the screen,
    then center it.

-s2e, --stretch2edge <image>
    Scale the image proportionally to cover the screen.
    The image may extend beyond the screen edges.


## Hackedbox Integration

hbsetbg is built as part of Hackedbox and is used internally by the
Hackedbox style/background system. Users normally do not need to invoke
hbsetbg directly.

Backgrounds are configured through the Hackedbox style configuration.
When a style specifies a root background, Hackedbox invokes hbsetbg
behind the scenes to apply the requested background.

Style properties include:

root.background
    Sets a single background image.

    Hackedbox uses Imlib2 to load the image, so supported image
    formats are determined by the available Imlib2 image loaders.

    Example:
        root.background: /path/to/image.jpg


root.background.mode
    Controls how the background image is displayed.

    The available modes are the four modes supported by hbsetbg:

        center
        tile
        s2c
        s2e


root.background.folder
    Specifies a folder containing background images.

    It is used together with root.background.timer to rotate
    backgrounds automatically.

    Example:
        root.background.folder: ~/.hackedbox/backgrounds


root.background.timer
    Sets the interval used when rotating backgrounds from
    root.background.folder.

    The value is specified in milliseconds.

    Example:
        root.background.timer: 300000

    300000 milliseconds = 5 minutes.


## Purpose

hbsetbg provides the actual background-setting functionality while
keeping background configuration inside the Hackedbox style system.

This separates:

Style configuration
    ↓
Hackedbox / HbTK style handling
    ↓
hbsetbg
    ↓
Root window


The utility therefore remains a separate executable within the
Hackedbox system, but is not intended to be part of the normal
user-facing configuration workflow.

## Status

hbsetbg was developed specifically for Hackedbox and provides the
background-setting functionality required by the Hackedbox style
system.

It removes the need for users to manually manage the root background
through external background-setting commands.

Direct invocation remains available for development and debugging,
but normal operation is handled automatically by Hackedbox.
